    hero: {
      title: 'PIŞTI',
      subtitle: 'Card game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Pişti',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Pişti (pronounced 'Pish-tee') is one of the most beloved card games in Turkey. It is a fishing game where players capture cards from a central pile by matching ranks or playing a Jack (which captures any pile). The name means 'cooked' and refers to the pişti bonus: capturing a single card with a matching rank scores 10 extra points. Fast-paced, with observation and memory.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Derived from ancient Central Asian card games and formalized in Turkey during the Ottoman era. It shares roots with the Spanish game 'Escoba'.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2 players (this variant). Also commonly 4 in partnerships or 4 individuals; rules are the same.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card pack (French suits, no jokers). Pişti uses the full deck: four cards are dealt face down to the centre, four to each player, and the remainder forms the stock; one centre card is turned face up to start the pile.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card pack (French suits, no jokers). Pişti uses the full deck: four cards are dealt face down to the centre, four to each player, and the remainder forms the stock; one centre card is turned face up to start the pile.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Dealer shuffles; player to dealer's left cuts. Dealer deals 4 cards face down to the center, then 4 to each player. Remaining cards form the stock. One of the four center cards is turned face up to start the pile (if it is a Jack, turn until a non-Jack). Player to dealer's right plays first. After all play their 4 cards, dealer deals 4 more to each from stock; repeat until stock is exhausted.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Four in partnerships: Four players in two partnerships, partners sitting opposite. Rules same; play anticlockwise. First team to 151 wins.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Four individuals: Four players each keeping individual score. Same rules; first to 151 wins.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Bluffing variation: When one card is on the table, next player may play a card face down claiming a pişti. If the first player believes it, 10 points; if not and it was a bluff, first player scores 20; if not and it matched, double pişti 20 to the captor.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Pişti (pronounced 'Pish-tee') is one of the most beloved card games in Turkey. It is a fishing game where players capture cards from a central pile by matching ranks or playing a Jack (which captures any pile). The name means 'cooked' and refers to the pişti bonus: capturing a single card with a matching rank scores 10 extra points. Fast-paced, with observation and memory.",
